and has a negative gradient everywhere) takes ﬁnite values at the ends of the interval over which the mass is distributed. As a result, the x-interval, occupied by the gas, shrinks in the course of evolution. Assuming for simplicity that ρ(m, 0) is an even function of m, we ﬁnd from Eqs. (15) and (21) that the interval [−L(t), L(t)], occupied by the gas, shrinks with time as L(t) = L(0)−2µt Z π/2µ 0 s A cos(µm′) ρ(m′, 0) dm′ +µAt2 (49) and reaches its minimum at t = tc. This minimum is al- ways positive except in the degenerate case of ρ(m, 0) = ρ0/ cos(µm), when the whole gas collapses into the point x = 0 at the time of singularity. It turns out that, in this degenerate case, the solution is self-similar in the Eule- rian coordinates x and t and separable in the Lagrangian coordinates m and t. It can be shown that all self-similar solutions with a ﬁnite energy have an inﬁnite density at some locations already at t = 0. Such initial conditions do not correspond to a dilute gas, so they will not be considered here. 9 A generic example of the solution on a ﬁnite Eule- rian interval is provided by the uniform initial density ρ(m, 0) = ρ0. In the Eulerian coordinates this choice corresponds to the constant initial density, ρ(x, 0) = ρ0, at the interval [−πl/2, πl/2]. The solutions (20)-(22) be- come p(m, t) = ρ0T0 cos(µm), (50) ρ(m, t) = ρ0 [1 −(t/τ) p cos(µm)]2 , (51) v(m, t) = − p 2T0  2E µm 2 2  −t τ sin(µm)  ,(52) where E (. . . | . . .) is the elliptic integral of the second kind. The relation for x(m, t) is the following: x(m, t) l = µm −4  t τ  E µm 2 2  +  t τ 2 sin(µm). Here the singularity occurs at x = 0 at time tc = τ whereas L(tc) > 0. The local structure of singularity is the same as in the case of an inﬁnite interval, see subsec- tion III F. E. Energy decay for the exact solutions A useful global characteristics of the clustering process is provided by the evolution of the total kinetic energy of all particles versus time, E(t) > 0. This quantity is con- venient to follow in experiment and in MD simulations. In the language of hydrodynamics it is E(t) = Z ∞ −∞  ρT γ −1 + ρv2 2  dx , (53) where the ﬁrst term under the integral is the thermal energy density, and the second term is the macroscopic kinetic energy density. Let us compute E(t) for the exact solutions Eqs. (20)-(22). First, consider the conditions under which the initial total energy E(0) is ﬁnite. For the initial thermal energy we have Eth(0) = Z ∞ −∞ ρ(x, 0)T (x, 0)dx γ −1 (54) = 2A Z π/2µ −π/2µ cos(µm)dm (γ −1)ρ(m, 0) . Whether this quantity is ﬁnite or not depends on the behavior of ρ(m, 0) near m = ±π/2µ. For example, as- suming as before that ρ(m, 0) ∼(π/2µ −m)1+a near m = π/2µ, we ﬁnd that Eth(0) is ﬁnite at a < 1 and inﬁnite otherwise. A simple example of the initial con- dition with an inﬁnite energy is ρ(m, 0) = ρ0 cos2(µm) corresponding to the Lorentzian initial density proﬁle ρ(x, 0) = ρ0/[1+(x/l)2] in the Eulerian coordinates. Here ρ(x, 0)T (x, 0) = ρ0T0/ p 1 + (x/l)2 , −∞< x < ∞, which is not integrable. Now, since at a < 2 the gas velocity is ﬁnite at m = π/2µ (see subsection III A), then at a < 1 the initial macroscopic kinetic energy Ekin(0) = Z ∞ −∞ ρv2 2 dx = Z π/2µ −π/2µ v2(m, 0) 2 dm (55) is also ﬁnite. Therefore, we assume a < 1 so that E(0) < ∞. Using the divergence form of the energy equation, ∂ ∂t  ρT γ −1 + ρv2 2  + ∂ ∂x γρvT γ −1 + ρv3 2  = −Λρ2T 3/2 γ −1 , (56) we can express the decay rate of the total energy as dE dt = − Λ γ −1 Z ∞ −∞ ρ2T 3/2dx = − Λ γ −1 Z π/2µ −π/2µ p3/2dm ρ1/2 . (57) Using Eqs. (20) and (21), we obtain dE dt = −Λ(2A)3/2 γ −1 ""Z π/2µ −π/2µ dm cos3/2(µm) p ρ(m, 0) −π √ At 2 # . (58) It is easy to see that the integral in this equation con- verges for the assumed behavior of the initial density. Therefore, the energy decays quadratically in time: E(t) = E(0) −Λ(2A)3/2t γ −1 Z π/2µ −π/2µ dm cos3/2(µm) p ρ(m, 0) + πΛA2t2 √ 2(γ −1). (59) One can check that the decay of the thermal and macro- sco