P ∈Rm and εP ∈R as follows. Write B(x, ε) for the open set of points in J whose distance from x is less than ε. Let x0 ∈U and ε0 ∈(0, 1) be such that B(x0, ε0) ⊆U. For each P ∈N, let xP +1 ∈U and εP +1 ∈(0, 2−P −1) be such that B(xP +1, εP +1) ⊆B(xP , εP ) \ JP . This is possible because B(xP , εP ) \ JP is open and nonempty, since JP is closed and has empty interior. As P tends to inﬁnity, xP converges to a point in U \ S P ∈N JP . The proof of Theorem 3 also uses the following Identity Theorem (for real analytic functions of several variables), also known as the Principle of Analytic Continuation. It can be proved by straightforwardly generalizing the same assertion for unary functions [10, Section 1.2]. Theorem 15. An analytic function with open connected domain that vanishes on an open set vanishes everywhere. Let J ⊆R be an open interval. It is well known that functions u0, . . . , uk−1 ∈Cω[J] are linearly dependent if and only if the determinant (Diuj)i,j=0,...,k−1 , called their Wronskian, is zero. Using this fact, Ritt and Gourin [17] showed (iiiR) ⇒(iii) of Theorem 3. Theorem 16. Let J ⊆R be an open interval and let f ∈Cω[J]. If we have P (f, D1f, D2f, . . . , Darity P −1f) = 0 (25) for some R-coeﬃcient nonzero polynomial P, then we have (25) for some Z-coeﬃcient nonzero polynomial P. Proof. By the assumption, there is a ﬁnite set B ⊆Narity P such that the functions f ν0 · (Df)ν1 · · · (Darity P −1f)νarity P −1, for (ν0, . . . , νarity P −1) ∈B, (26) are linearly dependent. The Wronskian of (26) thus vanishes, which is a Z-coeﬃcient polynomial in f, D1f, . . . , Darity P +|B|−1f. This polynomial is nonzero, since otherwise (26) would be linearly dependent for arbitrary f, which is absurd. One direction of Lemma 5 uses the following Transcendence Degree Theorem. Theorem 17. Let F be a subﬁeld of a ﬁeld E and D be a subset of E. If D ⊆F(B) for some ﬁnite set B ⊆E, then D ⊆F(C) for some ﬁnite set C ⊆D. Proof. For each d ∈D, the assumption gives dl = l−1 P j=0 βj · dj (27) 12 for some l ∈N \ {0} and βj ∈F(B). Suppose that for some d = d0 ∈D, this equation contains some b ∈B \ D, since otherwise we are done. Then we can rewrite (27) as bk = k−1 P i=0 αi · bi (28) for some k ∈N \ {0} and αi ∈F(B′), where B′ = (B \ {b}) ∪{d0}. For each d ∈D and t ∈N, we can substitute (27) and (28) repeatedly in dt to write dt = k−1 P i=0 l−1 P j=0 γi,j · ci · dj (29) for some γi,j ∈F(B′). The k · l + 1 elements 1, d, d2, . . . , dk·l are hence linearly dependent over F(B′). We have thus found a set B′ with D ⊆F(B′) such that B′ \ D has strictly less elements than B \ D. Repeat. B. Maximal unique function This section shows that, from a set K of functions with a certain property, we can choose a function of which all functions in K is a restriction. This was used to justify Deﬁnition 9 in the presence of non-analytic functions where Theorem 12 (a) does not apply. We say that a set I ⊆R is 0-convex if it is either the empty set or a possibly unbounded interval containing 0. Note that the union of 0-convex sets is 0-convex. We say that a set K of functions from R is consistent if for any t ∈R, the set { gt | g ∈K } has at most one element. In this case, the union of K means the unique function k such that dom h = S g∈K dom g and for each t ∈dom h, there is some g ∈K with ht = gt. Lemma 18. Let H be a set of functions from R with 0-convex domain. Then the set K of functions unique in H is consistent. Moreover, if its union belongs to H, it belongs to K. Proof. For the ﬁrst claim, suppose otherwise. Then there are functions k0, k1 ∈K and t ∈ dom k0 ∩dom k1 such that k0t ̸= k1t. This contradicts the fact that k0 is unique in H. For the second claim, suppose that the union k of K is not unique in H. That is, there are a function g ∈H and t ∈dom k ∩dom g such that gt ̸= kt. There is k0 ∈K for which t ∈dom k0. We have k0t = kt ̸= gt, contradicting the fact that k0 is unique in H. This lemma can be applied to H = Hv in the situation of Deﬁnition 9, because there the union of any consistent subset of Hv belongs to Hv. C. Iteration As we noted, the deﬁnition (24) of the operator mn is ambiguous, as it contains a subexpression f (v, t) that may be undeﬁned for some (v, t). So when is mn f v deﬁned? Possible answers include: (a) When t+ and t−are deﬁned. (b) When at least either t+ or t−is deﬁned; the condition t+ < −t−will be used only when both are deﬁned. And when is t+ (resp. t−) deﬁned? Possible answers include: 13 x = clk t 1 2 1 t x x = zigzag t 1 2 1 t x fv f 2v f 3v x = g(v, t) x = h(v, t) 1 2 v t x Figure 3: Simulating iteration f kv by real recursive functions. (i) When there is t ≥0 (resp. ≤0) such that f (v, t) = 0 and (v, τ) ∈dom f for all τ ∈R. (ii) When there is t ≥0 (resp. ≤0) such that f (v, t) = 0 and (v, τ) ∈dom f for all τ ∈[−t, t] (resp. [t, −t]). (iii) When there is t ≥0 (resp. ≤0) such that f (v, t) = 0 and (v, τ) ∈dom f for all τ ∈[0, t] (resp. [t, 0]). (iv) When there is t ≥0 (resp. ≤0) such that f (v, t) = 0. For (i), (ii) and (iii), we may also consider adding the phrase “except for some countably many isolated τ” (compare (c′) in Section 3.3). Moore’s informal explanation by a programming language [13, Section 7] seems to suggest (b) and (ii). However, without discussing which is the “right” deﬁnition of mn, we show that, whichever we choose, Lemma 13 holds. The following proof is consistent with any of the above 2 × 7 possible deﬁnitions. Proof of Lemma 13. Denote mn f v by µt. f (v, t). Let zero?x = µy. (x2 + y2) · (1 −y), (30) integer?x = zero? ␀sin (π · x)  , (31) roundx = x −µr. integer?(x −r), (32) so that (32) is the unique integer in (x −1/2, x + 1/2]. We get inv of (7) by invx = µt. x · (x · t −1). (33) The above four functions are total. Let digit(x, b, i) = round  x bi −1 2  −b · round  x bi+1 −1 2  (34) for b > 0, where bi = exp(i · ln b). When b > 1 and i are integers, digit(x, b, i) is the digit in bi’s place when x is written in base-b notation. Deﬁne clkt = digit(t,