) (% ) with A′k ≤ ṕ. 2) Ci, Cj, Ck make (1″) hold with A′k ≤ ṕ in all probability. Proof: 1) Refuting the Pseudo Attack on the REESSE1+ Cryptosystem http://arxiv.org/pdf/0704.0492 9 Let Οd be an oracle for a discrete logarithm. Suppose that W ′ ∈ [1, ] is a generator of ( * M , ·). In terms of group theories, ∀ A′k ∈ {2, …, ṕ}, the equation Ck ≡ A′k W ′ f ′ (k) (% M) has a solution. f ′(k) may be taken through Οd. ∀ f ′(i) ∈ [1, ], and let f ′(j) ≡ f ′(k) – f ′(i) (% ). Then, from Ci ≡ A′i W ′ f ′ (i) and Cj ≡ A′j W ′ f ′ (j) (% M), we can obtain many distinct pairs (A′i, A′j), where A′i, A′j ∈ (1, M), and f ′(i) + f ′(j) ≡ f ′(k) (% ). 2) Let Z ≡ Ci Cj Ck –1 ≡ A′i A′j W ′ f ′ (i) + f ′ (j) (A′k W ′ f ′ (k))–1 (% M) with f ′(i) + f ′(j) ≡ f ′(k) (% ) but f(i) + f(j) ≠ f(k). Further, there is A′i A′j ≡ Ci Cj Ck –1 A′k (% M). It is easily seen from the above equations the values of W′ and f ′(k) do not influence the value of A′i A′j. If A′k ∈ [2, ṕ] changes, A′i A′j also changes. Thus, ∀ i, j, k ∈ [1, n], the number of value of A′i A′j is ṕ – 1. Let M = 2 q ṕ 2 A′k, where q is a rational number. According to (1), Z / M – L / A′k = A′i A′j / (M A′k) = A′i A′j / (2q ṕ 2 A′k 2). When A′i A′j ≤ q ṕ 2, there is Z / M – L / A′k ≤ q ṕ 2 / (2 q ṕ 2 A′k 2) = 1 / (2 A′k 2) which satisfies (1″). Assume that the value of A′i A′j distributes uniformly on (1, M). Then, the probability that A′i A′j makes (1″) hold is P∀ i, j, k ∈ [1, n] = (q ṕ 2 / (2 q ṕ 2)) (1 / 2 + … + 1 / ṕ)) ≥ (1 / 2)(2 (ṕ – 1) / (ṕ + 2)) = 1 – 3 / (ṕ + 2). It is seen that the probability is very large. According to property I.2, for a certain Ck ∈ {C1, …, Cn} and ∀ Ci, Cj ∈ {C1, …, Cn}, Ak will have roughly n2 values by (1″) namely the condition at fact 1.1, including the repeated, and considering the symmetry, almost every value has at least one counterpart. Of course, if the condition at fact 4, namely qu+1 > qu ∆ which connotes (1″) is used as a constraint, the number of values of Ak = qu will decrease