0704.0695,Photon splitting in a laser field,"Photon splitting due to vacuum polarization in a laser field is considered. Using an operator technique, we derive the amplitudes for arbitrary strength, spectral content and polarization of the laser field. The case of a monochromatic circularly polarized laser field is studied in detail and the amplitudes are obtained as three-fold integrals. The asymptotic behavior of the amplitudes for various limits of interest are investigated also in the case of a linearly polarized laser field. Using the obtained results, the possibility of experimental observation of the process is discussed.","arXiv:0704.0695v2 [hep-ph] 8 Sep 2007 Photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld A. Di Piazza,1, ∗A. I. Milstein,1, 2, † and C. H. Keitel1, ‡ 1Max-Planck-Institut f¨ur Kernphysik, Saupfercheckweg 1, 69117 Heidelberg, Germany 2Budker Institute of Nuclear Physics, 630090 Novosibirsk, Russia (Dated: November 3, 2018) Abstract Photon splitting due to vacuum polarization in a laser ﬁeld is considered. Using an operator technique, we derive the amplitudes for arbitrary strength, spectral content and polarization of the laser ﬁeld. The case of a monochromatic circularly polarized laser ﬁeld is studied in detail and the amplitudes are obtained as three-fold integrals. The asymptotic behavior of the amplitudes for various limits of interest are investigated also in the case of a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld. Using the obtained results, the possibility of experimental observation of the process is discussed. PACS numbers: 12.20.Ds (QED Speciﬁc Calculations), 42.62.-b (Laser Applications), 42.50.Xa (Optical Tests in Quantum Electrodynamics) ∗Electronic address: dipiazza@mpi-hd.mpg.de †Electronic address: milstein@inp.nsk.su ‡Electronic address: keitel@mpi-hd.mpg.de 1 I. INTRODUCTION Vacuum polarization is known to result in such nonlinear Quantum Electrodynamics (QED) eﬀects as coherent photon scattering and photon splitting in an external electromag- netic ﬁeld. Photon scattering in the electric ﬁeld of atoms (Delbr¨uck scattering) has been investigated in detail both theoretically and experimentally [1, 2, 3, 4]. Recently, an essen- tial progress in the understanding of the photon splitting process in an atomic electric ﬁeld was achieved [5, 6] and the ﬁrst successful observation of photon splitting was performed [7]. It turned out that higher orders of the perturbation theory with respect to Zα play an important role and drastically modify the cross sections of Delbr¨uck scattering and photon splitting at high photon energy (here, Z is the nuclear charge number, α = e2 = 1/137 is the ﬁne-structure constant with e being the electron charge, the system of units ℏ= c = 1 is used). Therefore, the theoretical and experimental investigation of photon splitting gives the possibility to verify QED in the presence of strong external ﬁeld when the nonperturbative eﬀects in the ﬁeld strength (contributions of higher-order terms) are very important. Photon splitting in a constant and uniform electromagnetic ﬁeld for arbitrary values of the ﬁeld strength was considered in numerous papers because of the potential astrophysical implications of the process (see, e.g., Refs. [8, 9, 10, 11, 12]). The possibility of observing photon splitting in the electric ﬁelds of single crystals at high photon energies was investi- gated in [13]. Photon splitting due to vacuum polarization in a strongly magnetized plasma was discussed in [14]. Photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld with arbitrary strength and frequency content has never been considered before for an arbitrary energy of the incoming photon. The kinematics of photon splitting depends essentially on the structure of the external ﬁeld. In the Coulomb ﬁeld, the wave vector k1 of the initial photon and the wave vectors k2 and k3 of the ﬁnal ones are, in general, not coplanar. In a constant and uniform electromagnetic ﬁeld, the initial and ﬁnal photons are almost collinear (up to nonzero photon mass due to vacuum polarization in the external ﬁeld). In a classical electromagnetic plane wave, due to energy-momentum conservation, the initial and ﬁnal photons are coplanar in the frame where the laser wave propagates in the direction anti-parallel to k1. Throughout the paper we refer to this frame as the laboratory frame. The structure of the photon splitting amplitude in a laser ﬁeld depends on the value of two Lorentz-invariant parameters. In the laboratory frame they are 2 given by η = ω1ω0 m2 , χ = ω1 m E Ec , (1) where ω1 is the energy of the initial photon, ω0 and E are the characteristic frequency and electric ﬁeld strength of the laser pulse, Ec = m2/|e| = 1.3 × 1016 V/cm is the critical electric ﬁeld and m is the electron mass. For a monochromatic laser ﬁeld, ω0 and E in Eq. (1) are the laser frequency and the root mean square of the laser electric ﬁeld. For η ≪1 and χ ≪1, photon splitting in a monochromatic laser wave was considered in [15] in the leading approximation with the help of the Euler-Heisenberg Lagrangian. The contribution to the amplitudes of photon splitting linear in χ but at arbitrary η is nothing but the usual photon-photon scattering which was studied in detail theoretically (see, e.g., [16]) but never observed experimentally [17]. The possibility to observe “laser-assisted” photon-photon scattering by using the next generation of petawatt lasers has been investigated in [18, 19]. Other processes related to photon-photon scattering and initiated by vacuum polarization in the presence of the superposition of two laser ﬁelds, or a laser ﬁeld and a Coulomb ﬁeld have been considered in [20, 21, 22, 23]. For the strongest optical (ω0 ≈1 eV) laser beam available today the laser intensity is I ≈1022 W/cm2 and the corresponding electric ﬁeld amplitude is E ≈10−4Ec [24]. The Extreme Light Infrastructure (ELI) project aims at reaching laser intensity values higher than 1025 W/cm2 corresponding to E ≈10−2Ec. The X-ray Free-Electron Laser (X-FEL) facilities presently being developed at DESY (Hamburg, Germany) and at SLAC (Stanford, USA) are intended to provide spatially coherent and highly brilliant beams of synchrotron radiation with single-photon energies up to 8÷12 keV at a maximum intensity ≈1015W/cm2 corresponding to a ﬁeld strength of E ≈10−7Ec [26]. The above mentioned projects have ini- tiated intensive investigations of various QED processes that can be experimentally observed when using these future facilities [27, 28, 29]. On the other hand, sources of high-energy photons are nowadays available in a wide range of photon energies spanning from tens of MeV up to hundreds of GeV. It will then be possible to reach experimentally unexplored regions for the values of the parameters η and χ where vacuum polarization eﬀects in a laser ﬁeld, and photon splitting in particular, are essentially nonperturbative. In the present paper, we derive the amplitudes of photon splitting in a plane wave described by an arbitrary four-vector potential Aµ(φ) = (0, A(φ)), where φ = κx with κ = (1, κ), κ2 = 0, and κ · A(φ) = 0. At a ﬁrst stage, we do not assume that the plane 3 wave is monochromatic or even periodic. It is convenient, without loss of generality, to consider the process in the laboratory frame, i.e. in the frame where k1 is anti-parallel to the vector κ. The calculations are performed by using the operator technique developed in Refs. [30, 31] for the calculation of the polarization and mass operators in a laser ﬁeld. Alternative forms of these operators were derived independently in Refs. [32, 33] by means of a diﬀerent method. The results of Refs. [30, 31] and Refs. [32, 33] are in agreement with each other. The operator technique allows one to ﬁnd the amplitudes without using the explicit form of the Green’s function for the Dirac equation in the external ﬁeld. This circumstance considerably simpliﬁes the calculations. The rest of the paper is organized as follows: in Section II we describe the general prop- erties of the photon splitting amplitudes. Then, starting from the operator representation of the amplitudes, in Sections III and IV we calculate the explicit expressions of the amplitudes for a general vector potential of the form A(φ). In Section V we consider a monochromatic laser ﬁeld with particular emphasis on the case of a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld. In Section VI we analyze various asymptotic forms of the amplitudes both for a circular and for a lin- early polarized ﬁeld. The possibility of the experimental observation of photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld is discussed in detail in Section VII. Finally, in Section VIII the main conclusions of the paper are presented. II. GENERAL DISCUSSION The amplitude M of the photon splitting process of a photon with four-momentum k1 = (ω1, k1) into two photons with four-momenta k2 = (ω2, k2) and k3 = (ω3, k3) with k2 1 = k2 2 = k2 3 = 0 is represented by the Feynman diagram shown in Fig. 1. The corresponding formal expression for M in the Furry representation reads M = ie3 Z d4x Tr⟨x|e−ik1x ˆe1 1 ˆP −m + i0 eik2x ˆe∗ 2 1 ˆP −m + i0 eik3x ˆe∗ 3 1 ˆP −m + i0 |x⟩ +(k2 ↔k3 , e2 ↔e3) , (2) where e1µ is the initial photon polarization four-vector, e2µ and e3µ are the polarization four-vectors of the ﬁnal photons, ˆP = Pµγµ with Pµ = i∂µ −eAµ(x) and γµ being the Dirac matrices. The factor (4π)3/2, contained in the external photon wave functions, has been included into the formula for the probability of the process. The calculation of the amplitude 4 M is essentially simpliﬁed by using the Green’s function D(x2, x1) of the “squared” Dirac equation: D(x2, x1) = ⟨x2| 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 |x1⟩. (3) As shown in [5], the expression of M can be written as a sum of two contributions containing either three or two Green’s functions D(x2, x1): M ≡Mµνρe1µe∗ 2νe∗ 3ρ = M(2) + M(3) . (4) The term M(2) has the form M(2) = M(2) 1 + M(2) 2 + M(2) 3 (5) M(2) 1 = −ie3(e∗ 2e∗ 3) Z d4xTr⟨x|e−ik1x(2e1P −ˆe1ˆk1) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 ei(k2+k3)x 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 |x⟩, M(2) 2 = −ie3(e1e∗ 3) Z d4xTr⟨x|eik2x(2e∗ 2P + ˆe∗ 2ˆk2) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 ei(k3−k1)x 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 |x⟩, M(2) 3 = −ie3(e1e∗ 2) Z d4xTr⟨x|eik3x(2e∗ 3P + ˆe∗ 3ˆk3) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 ei(k2−k1)x 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 |x⟩. The term M(3) is given by M(3) = i 2e3 Z dx Tr⟨x|e−ik1x(−ˆe1ˆk1 + 2e1P) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 ×eik2x(ˆe∗ 2ˆk2 + 2e∗ 2P) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 eik3x(ˆe∗ 3ˆk3 + 2e∗ 3P) 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 |x⟩ +(k2 ↔k3 , e2 ↔e3) . (6) For each photon with the four-momentum kµ i (i = 1, 2, 3) it is convenient to introduce two vectors εµ i,λi, (λi = 1, 2) εµ i,λi = aµ λi −kiaλi kiκ κµ , εi,λiεj,λj = −δλiλj a0 λ = 0 , (aλ)2 = 1 , a1 · a2 = 0 , aλ · κ = 0. (7) Due to gauge invariance, it is possible to write the tensor Mµνρ introduced in Eq. (4) for real photons in the form (see Ref. [30]): Mµνρ = − X λ1,λ2λ3=1,2 Rλ1λ2λ3εµ 1,λ1εν 2,λ2ερ 3,λ3 , (8) For the coeﬃcients Rλ1λ2λ3 we have Rλ1λ2λ3 = Mµνρεµ 1,λ1εν 2,λ2ερ 3,λ3 . (9) 5 So, the coeﬃcients Rλ1λ2λ3 can be calculated from Eqs. (5) and (6) by the substitution eµ i −→εµ i,λi. Using the coeﬃcients Rλ1λ2λ3, we write the amplitude M of photon splitting in the laser ﬁeld as M = − X λ1,λ2λ3=1,2 Rλ1λ2λ3(ε1,λ1e1)(ε2,λ2e∗ 2)(ε3,λ3e∗ 3) . (10) This expression of the amplitude M is very convenient because it automatically provides the fulﬁllment of the gauge invariance principle. As a consequence, it is not necessary to perform the subtraction in the calculation of the lowest-order photon splitting amplitude, described by the photon-photon scattering diagrams. We perform the calculations in terms of the helicity amplitudes Mσ1σ2σ3, with σj = ±1. In this case, the polarization vector eσ of each external photon with the wave vector k and the energy ω satisﬁes the relations eσ · k = 0 and eσ × k = iσωeσ. Due to momentum conservation in the laser ﬁeld, the ﬁnal transverse momenta k2⊥and k3⊥(with respect to the laser propagation direction) are equal in modulus and opposite in direction: k2⊥= −k3⊥≡q and q · κ = q · k1 = 0. We direct the vector a1 along the vector q, so that a2 = κ × a1, then e1 ,σ1 = −1 √ 2 (a2 + iσ1a1) , e2 ,σ2 = 1 √ 2(a2 −iσ2a2 × k2/ω2) , e3 ,σ3 = 1 √ 2 (a2 −iσ3a2 × k3/ω3) . (11) The corresponding polarization four-vectors ei,σi = (0, ei ,σi) have the following products with the four-vectors εi,λi: ε1,λ1e1,σ1 = 1 √ 2(iσ1δλ1,1 + δλ1,2) , ε2,λ2e2,σ2 = 1 √ 2(iσ2δλ2,1 −δλ2,2) , ε3,λ3e3,σ3 = 1 √ 2 (iσ3δλ3,1 −δλ3,2) . (12) These useful relations allow us to state that the amplitude Mσ1σ2σ3 can be obtained from the coeﬃcient Rλ1λ2λ3 by the substitution in the latter: aλ1 →−1 √ 2(iσ1a1 + a2) , aλ2 →1 √ 2(−iσ2a1 + a2) , aλ3 →1 √ 2(−iσ3a1 + a2) . (13) 6 The energies ω2,3 and the parallel components k2,3 ∥= κ · k2,3 of the momenta of the ﬁnal photons are functions of the transverse momentum q and of the energy Ωabsorbed from the laser ﬁeld, Ω= ω2 + ω3 −ω1. It is convenient to introduce the quantities ν2,3 = κk2,3 κk1 = 1 ∓cos θ0 2 , cos θ0 = s 1 −q2 ω1Ω. (14) The angle θ0 coincides with the angle between the vectors k2 and κ in the frame where k2 = −k3, so that q2 ≤ω1Ω. Then ω2 = ω1ν2 + Ων3 , k2∥= −ω1ν2 + Ων3 ω3 = ω1ν3 + Ων2 , k3∥= −ω1ν3 + Ων2 . (15) It follows from Eqs. (5) and (6) that, like the amplitude M, the coeﬃcients Rλ1λ2λ3 can also be presented as the sum Rλ1λ2λ3 = R(2) λ1λ2λ3 +R(3) λ1λ2λ3. In order to explain the application of the operator technique to the calculation of the photon splitting amplitude, we present the derivation of the contribution R(2) λ1λ2λ3. The calculation of R(3) λ1λ2λ3 can be performed in a similar way and only the ﬁnal result is given for this contribution. III. CALCULATION OF R(2) λ1λ2λ3 We direct the vector κ along the z-axis and pass from the variables t and z to the variables φ = κx = t −z and T = (t + z)/2. In this way p0 = i∂t = −pφ −pT/2 , pz = −i∂z = −pφ + pT/2 , pφ = −i∂φ , pT = −i∂T . Then, we use in Eq. (5) the representation of the operator Green’s function derived in [31] 1 ˆP2 −m2 + i0 = −i Z ∞ 0 ds exp[is( ˆP2 −m2)] = −i Z ∞ 0 ds exp(−ism2)  1 + eˆκ 2pT [ ˆA(φ + 2spT) −ˆA(φ)]  × exp  −i Z s 0 ds′ (p⊥−eA(φ + 2s′pT))2  exp(2ipφpT) . (16) As before, the index ⊥indicates transverse quantities with respect to the laser propagation direction κ. Now, we pass in Eq. (5) from the integration over the variables T and x⊥to the corresponding momenta pT and p⊥in accordance with Z dT⟨T|...|T⟩→ Z dpT 2π ⟨pT|...|pT⟩, Z dx⊥⟨x⊥|...|x⊥⟩→ Z dp⊥ (2π)2⟨p⊥|...|p⊥⟩. 7 We also exploit the relations exp(2ispφpT)g(φ) exp(−2ispφpT) = g(φ + 2spT) , exp(2ispφpT)|φ⟩= |φ −2spT⟩, (17) valid for an arbitrary function g(φ) of the variable φ. Besides, the matrix elements ⟨pT|...|pT⟩ and ⟨p⊥|...|p⊥⟩become ⟨pT| exp[i2ω1T(ν2 + ν3 −1)|pT⟩= 2πδ ␀2ω1(ν2 + ν3 −1)  , ⟨p⊥| exp[−ix⊥· (k2⊥+ k3⊥)]|p⊥⟩= (2π)2δ(k2⊥+ k3⊥) . (18) The factors (18) are common to all amplitudes. They will be omitted below in the expres- sions of the amplitudes and will be taken into account in the usual way in the formula for the probability of the process. The matrix element ⟨φ|...|φ⟩also becomes the δ-function con- taining the variable pT in its argument. Therefore, the integration over pT becomes trivial as well as the integration over p⊥. Following the procedure pointed out above and using the useful relations kµ 2 = Ων3κµ + ν2kµ 1 + qµ , kµ 3 = Ων2κµ + ν3kµ 1 −qµ , k2k3 = 2Ωω1 , k2k1 = 2Ωω1ν3 , k3k1 = 2Ωω1ν2 , q2 = 4Ωω1ν2ν3 , (19) with qµ = (0, q), we arrive at the following result for R(2) λ1λ2λ3: R(2) λ1λ2λ3 = −me3 (2π)2 X j=1,2,3 δ(j) ∞ ZZ 0 ds1 ds2 (s1 + s2)2 exp[−i(s1 + s2)] × Z dφ exp ( iΩφ + i4ω1Ω m2 ϕj −i(s1 + s2) ""Z 1 0 dy∆2(yϕj) − Z 1 0 dy∆(yϕj) 2#) ×  −2aλj · Z 1 0 dy∆(yϕj) + (s1 + s2)2 2s1s2 aλj · ∆(ϕj)  . (20) Here we deﬁned the quantities δ(1) = δλ2λ3 , δ(2) = δλ1λ3 , δ(3) = δλ1λ2 , (ϕ1, ϕ2, ϕ3) = s1s2 s1 + s2 (1, −ν2, −ν3) , ∆(u) = e m[A(φ + 4uω1/m2) −A(φ)] . (21) In the parametrization of the Green’s function we made the substitution s →s/m2. As it has to be according to the Furry theorem, the contribution (20) contains only odd powers of the external ﬁeld. The explicit result of the integration over φ depends on the form of the function A(φ). For a monochromatic wave, the integral over φ can be easily performed (see Section V). 8 IV. CALCULATION OF R(3) λ1λ2λ3 The calculation of the contribution R(3) λ1λ2λ3 is similar to that of R(2) λ1λ2λ3, but considerably more cumbersome. In order to bring the answer into a relatively short form, we introduce the convenient notation S = s1 + s2 + s3 , τ1 = s2ν2 −s3ν3 S , τ2 = ν2 −τ1 , τ3 = ν3 + τ1 , D1(y) = ∆(s2τ2y + s3τ3(1 −y)) , D2(y) = ∆(s2τ2y) , D3(y) = ∆(s3τ3y) , F = 1 S Z 1 0 dy[s1D1(y) + s2D2(y) + s3D3(y)] , F0 = SF 2 − Z 1 0 dy[s1D2 1(y) + s2D2 2(y) + s3D2 3(y)] , G = 2s1 S Z 1 0 dy [s2(D1(y) −D2(y)) + s3(D1(y) −D3(y))] , (22) and use the relation s1τ1 = s2τ2 −s3τ3. Before taking the trace over the γ-matrices, we obtain the following compact representation for R(3) λ1λ2λ3: R(3) λ1λ2λ3 = ime3 8π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 S2 Z dφ exp  i  Ωφ + 4ω1Ωs2s3 m2S + F0 −S + ρ · G  × ( 1 4Tr "" −2aλ3 · N3 + ˆε3,λ3ˆk3 m ! 1 + mˆκ ˆd3 2ω1 ! −2aλ1 · N1 −ˆε1,λ1ˆk1 m ! × 1 + mˆκ ˆd2 2ω1 ! −2aλ2 · N2 + ˆε2,λ2ˆk2 m ! 1 + mˆκ ˆd1 2ω1 ! # +4i S  δ(1)aλ1 · (N1 −V ) + δ(2)aλ2 · (N2 + ν2V ) + δ(3)aλ3 · (N3 + ν3V )  ) +(2 ↔3 , ρ →−ρ) , (23) where ρ = q m , N1 = F −s1ρ S , N2 = F −D2(1) + s3ρ Sν2 , N3 = F −D3(1) + s2ρ Sν3 , V = 1 2(d1 + d2 + d3) , d1 = D2(1) −D3(1) 2τ1 , d2 = D2(1) 2τ2 , d3 = D3(1) 2τ3 . (24) The substitution (2 ↔3 , ρ →−ρ) in Eq. (23) results in the replacement N1(ρ) → N1(−ρ), N2(ρ) →N3(−ρ), and N3(ρ) →N2(−ρ). In fact, the role of the term with the 9 substitution (2 ↔3 , ρ →−ρ) is to provide the fulﬁllment of the Furry theorem (R(3) λ1λ2λ3 contains only odd powers of the external ﬁeld). Therefore, the sum of the two terms is twice the odd part of the ﬁrst term with respect to the external vector potential A(φ). Finally, we take the trace and obtain: R(3) λ1λ2λ3 = ime3 4π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 S2 Z dφ exp  i  Ωφ + 4ω1Ωs2s3 m2S + F0 −S + ρ · G  × * −4(aλ1 · N1)(aλ2 · N2)(aλ3 · N3) + 4(aλ2 · N2)(aλ3 · N3)(aλ1 · V ) −4ν3(aλ1 · N1)(aλ2 · N2)(aλ3 · V ) −4ν2(aλ1 · N1)(aλ3 · N3)(aλ2 · V ) +(aλ1 · N1) [ρ · ρ]λ2λ3 2ν2ν3 + [ρ · (d1 −d2 −d3)]λ2λ3 −2ν2ν3[d1 · (d2 + d3)]λ2λ3  −(aλ2 · N2) [ρ · ρ]λ1λ3 2ν3 + [ρ · (d1 + d2 −d3)]λ1λ3 −2ν3[d3 · (d1 + d2)]λ1λ3  −(aλ3 · N3) [ρ · ρ]λ1λ2 2ν2 + [ρ · (d1 −d2 + d3)]λ1λ2 −2ν2[d2 · (d1 + d3)]λ1λ2  +(aλ1 · ρ)[ρ · d1]λ2λ3 −1 ν2 (aλ2 · ρ)[ρ · d3]λ1λ3 −1 ν3 (aλ3 · ρ)[ρ · d2]λ1λ2 −ρ2 2  [aλ1 · d1]λ2λ3 −1 ν2 [aλ2 · d3]λ1λ3 −1 ν3 [aλ3 · d2]λ1λ2  + 1 ν2ν3 (aλ1 · ρ)(aλ2 · ρ)(aλ3 · ρ) − ρ2 4ν2ν3 [δ(1)(aλ1 · ρ) + δ(2)(aλ2 · ρ) + δ(3)(aλ3 · ρ)] +2i S  δ(1)aλ1 · (N1 −V ) + δ(2)aλ2 · (N2 + ν2V ) + δ(3)aλ3 · (N3 + ν3V )  +U1 −ν2 U2 −ν3 U3 + 2ν2ν3 U4 + + (2 ↔3 , ρ →−ρ) . (25) Here, we introduced the operator [X · Y ]λiλj ≡(X · aλi)(Y · aλj) + (X · aλj)(Y · aλi) −(X · Y )(aλi · aλj) (26) 10 for any two vectors X and Y and we used the abbreviations U1 = −(aλ1 · ρ)[d2 · d3]λ2λ3 + (aλ2 · ρ)[d2 · d3]λ1λ3 + (aλ3 · ρ)[d2 · d3]λ1λ2 +2(aλ1 · ρ)(aλ2 · d2)(aλ3 · d3) −(d2 · ρ)[d3 · aλ2]λ1λ3 −(d3 · ρ)[d2 · aλ3]λ1λ2 , U2 = (aλ1 · ρ)[d1 · d2]λ2λ3 −(aλ2 · ρ)[d1 · d2]λ1λ3 + (aλ3 · ρ)[d1 · d2]λ1λ2 +2(aλ2 · ρ)(aλ1 · d2)(aλ3 · d1) −(d1 · ρ)[d2 · aλ3]λ1λ2 −(d2 · ρ)[d1 · aλ1]λ2λ3 , U3 = (aλ1 · ρ)[d1 · d3]λ2λ3 + (aλ2 · ρ)[d1 · d3]λ1λ3 −(aλ3 · ρ)[d1 · d3]λ1λ2 +2(aλ3 · ρ)(aλ1 · d3)(aλ2 · d1) −(d1 · ρ)[d3 · aλ2]λ1λ3 −(d3 · ρ)[d1 · aλ1]λ2λ3 , U4 = (aλ1 · d2)[d1 · d3]λ2λ3 + (aλ1 · d3)[d1 · d2]λ2λ3 −(aλ1 · d1)[d2 · d3]λ2λ3 +2(aλ1 · d1)(aλ2 · d2)(aλ3 · d3) + (d1 · d2)[d3 · (δ(2)aλ2 −δ(3)aλ3)] −(d1 · d3)[d2 · (δ(2)aλ2 −δ(3)aλ3)] −(d2 · d3)[d1 · (δ(2)aλ2 + δ(3)aλ3)] . (27) Equations (20) and (25) are valid for an arbitrary vector potential A(φ) and they are our starting point for further consideration. The simplest case A(φ) = −E0φ, where E0 is a constant vector perpendicular to κ, corresponds to the so-called crossed ﬁeld with the electric ﬁeld E = E0 and the magnetic ﬁeld H = κ × E0 , |H| = |E|. In this case there is no energy and momentum transfer from the ﬁeld so that Ω= 0 and q = 0 (the momenta k2 and k3 of the ﬁnal photons are parallel to the momentum k1 of the initial photon). Note that in this case the expansion of the amplitudes over E0/Ec starts with the terms of order of (E0/Ec)3 while the linear terms in E0/Ec vanish. We have checked that for a crossed ﬁeld our amplitudes agree with those found in [11]. V. PHOTON SPLITTING IN A MONOCHROMATIC PLANE WAVE Below, we consider the external ﬁeld to be a monochromatic plane wave with the fre- quency ω0. In this case, the helicity amplitude Mσ1σ2σ3 has the form Mσ1σ2σ3 = ∞ X′ n=1 2πδ(Ω−nω0)Mn, σ1σ2σ3 (28) 11 after the integration over the variable φ. In the above equation the prime means the sum- mation over odd numbers n and where Mn, σ1σ2σ3 denotes the invariant amplitude of the photon splitting process with absorption of n laser photons. Using the usual Fermi golden rule, we obtain for the photon splitting rate d ˙Wn d ˙Wn, σ1σ2σ3 = π ω1 |Mn, σ1σ2σ3|2 dq ω1Ωn cos θ0 = 2π ω1 |Mn, σ1σ2σ3|2 dν2 dφq , (29) where Ωn = nω0, cos θ0 is deﬁned in Eq. (14), and φq is the azimuth angle of the vector q in the plane perpendicular to κ. We remind the reader of the fact that q is a two- dimensional vector perpendicular to κ and k1. Besides, dq/(ω1Ωn cos θ0) coincides with the diﬀerential of the solid angle of the vector k2 in the frame where ω1 = Ωn. It is also useful to express the rate d ˙Wn, σ1σ2σ3 in terms of the polar angle θ2 = ∠(k2, κ) (or of the polar angle θ3 = ∠(k3, κ)). Using the expressions (15) valid for any Ωwe obtain the relations ν2,3 = 1 1 + ω1 Ωcot2(θ2,3/2) , cot(θ2/2) cot(θ3/2) = Ω ω1 . (30) As a result, we ﬁnd from Eq. (29) that d ˙Wn, σ1σ2σ3 = π Ωn |Mn, σ1σ2σ3|2 sin θ2 dθ2 dφq  sin2(θ2/2) + ω1 Ωn cos2(θ2/2) 2 . (31) In the general case of an elliptically polarized and monochromatic laser wave, the vector potential reads A(φ) = A1 cos(ω0φ) + A2 sin(ω0φ) , (32) where A1 · A2 = A1 · κ = A2 · κ = 0. Generally speaking, the vectors A1 and A2 are not parallel to the unit vectors a1 and a2 introduced in Eq. (7), respectively, as there is an azimuth asymmetry of the photon splitting amplitude for an elliptically polarized laser wave (we point out that a1 has been chosen to be parallel to q). However, in a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld it is |A1| = |A2|, then the photon splitting rate is symmetric with respect to the azimuth angle φq of the vector q, and the vector A1 can be directed along a1. The photon splitting rate can be converted into a cross section according to the relation dσ = d ˙W 2Φ , Φ = 1 8πω0(A2 1 + A2 2) = ω0m2 8πα (ξ2 1 + ξ2 2) , (33) with Φ being the laser photon ﬂux and ξ1,2 = eA1,2/m. Note that this cross section has a restricted meaning because in the presence of a strong ﬁeld it still depends on the laser 12 ﬂux through the parameters ξ1,2. In terms of the parameters ξ1,2 and η, the parameter χ introduced in Eq. (1) has the form χ = η p (ξ2 1 + ξ2 2)/2. The integral over φ in Eqs. (20) and (25) can be taken analytically for an elliptically polarized laser wave. However, the result is very cumbersome even in the case of linear laser polarization. This is why we limit ourselves to the case of a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld. In this case the answer is essentially simpler (but not simple). Thus we set |A1| = |A2| = A, ξ1 = ξ2 = ξ = eA/m and direct A1 along a1 and A2 along a2 (for positive helicity (p. h.)) or A2 along −a2 (for negative helicity (n. h.)). Only the case of positive helicity of the laser ﬁeld will be considered below and the amplitudes for negative helicity of the laser ﬁeld polarization can be obtained by means of the relation Mσ1σ2σ3(n. h.) = −M¯σ1¯σ2¯σ3(p. h.), where ¯σi denotes the helicity opposite to σi. Now, by using the substitution rule of Eqs. (13) and by carrying out the change of variables s1 = us and s2 = (1 −u)s in Eq. (20), we obtain for the nonzero contributions of M(2) n,σ1σ2σ3 the following result M(2) n,++−= G(1) + G(−ν3) , M(2) n,+−+ = G(1) + G(−ν2) , M(2) n,−−−= G(−ν2) + G(−ν3) , G(ν) = δn1 mξe3 4 √ 2π2 Z ∞ 0 ds s Z 1 0 du exp  −is  1 + ξ2  1 −sin2 ϑ ϑ2  ×eiϑ  2i  eiϑ −sin ϑ ϑ  + sin ϑ u(1 −u)  ; ϑ = 2u(1 −u)sην . (34) In order to write M(3) n,σ1σ2σ3 in a compact form, we introduce the following deﬁnitions fj = sin(2ηsjτj)ei2ηsjτj , ζ = i τ1η  f2 s2τ2 −f3 s3τ3  , φ0 = arg ζ , F0 = ξ2 4η2Sτ1τ2τ3 ν2ν3 τ1 |f1|2 + ν2 τ2 |f2|2 −ν3 τ3 |f3|2  −ξ2S , Z = ξ|ζ|ρns2s3 S , ρn = 2√nην2ν3 , S = s1 + s2 + s3 . (35) In terms of these functions, the contribution M(3) n,σ1σ2σ3 has the form M(3) n,σ1σ2σ3 = me3 4 √ 2π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 S2 exp h i  4nηs2s3 S + F0 −S i × 3 X j=−3 e−i(n+j)φ0 Jn+j(Z)Bj,σ1σ2σ3 , (36) where Jl(Z) are ordinary Bessel functions. The coeﬃcients Bj,σ1σ2σ3 are expressed via the functions fj introduced in Eq. (35) and they are presented in the Appendix. The expressions 13 (34) and (36) are valid for any value of the parameters η and ξ = χ/η. As it is clear from Eq. (36), the photon splitting amplitudes are not equal to zero for any number of absorbed laser photons. This is because the angle between the two ﬁnal photons is not zero (ρn ̸= 0) and the conservation of the projection Jz of the total angular momentum does not imply any selection rule. Note that the expansion of the amplitude Mn,σ1σ2σ3 = M(2) n,σ1σ2σ3 + M(3) n,σ1σ2σ3 for a ﬁxed n (see Eqs. (34) and (36)), contains in general all odd powers k of the parameter χ (or, equivalently, ξ) starting from k = n. The terms with k > n in Mn,σ1σ2σ3 correspond to rescattering processes of absorption and emission of laser photons with a net absorption of n laser photons. In the next Section we consider the asymptotics of the amplitudes M(2) n,σ1σ2σ3 and M(3) n,σ1σ2σ3 in some limiting cases. VI. ASYMPTOTICS OF THE AMPLITUDES Various limiting cases of the photon splitting amplitudes for a monochromatic plane wave are studied in this Section. A. Amplitudes for small η and small χ We ﬁrst consider the case of both small η and χ, η ≪1 and χ ≪1. Then, the leading contribution to the amplitudes is determined by the ratio η/χ2 [15]. For η/χ2 ≫1, the nonzero amplitudes in the leading approximation are given by the linear terms in χ in Eqs.(34) and (36) and correspond to n = 1. We obtain M1,+++ = 11N , M1,+−−= −2(1 −ν2ν3)N , M1,−−+ = 11ν2 2N , M1,−+−= 11ν2 3N , N = −ime3ηχ 45 √ 2π2 . (37) These amplitudes are nothing but the usual photon-photon scattering amplitudes for small Mandelstam variables s = 4ηm2, t = −4ην2m2 and u = −4ην3m2 (see, e.g., [16]). For η/χ2 ≪1, the leading contributions are proportional to χ3 and correspond to both n = 1 and n = 3. The nonzero amplitudes in the leading approximation are given by M1,+−+ = M1,++−= M1,−−−= −i37 15 e N , M3,+−−= i e N , e N = 2 √ 2me3χ3ν2ν3 21π2 . (38) 14 These results are in agreement with those obtained in [15]. B. Amplitudes for small η and ﬁxed χ We proceed now to the region of parameters η ≪1 and χ ﬁxed. In this case the leading terms of the amplitudes in a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld contain all powers of χ but only for n = 1 and n = 3 they still are not equal to zero. The reason is the following: at η ≪1, in a frame where ω1 ≫ω0, the vectors k2 and k3 are almost parallel to k1 and anti-parallel to κ. Since the clockwise polarized laser ﬁeld contains photons with projection of the total angular momentum Jz = +1 then, due to the conservation of Jz, only the values n = 1 and n = 3 are allowed and only the amplitudes M1,++−, M1,+−+, M1,−−−and M3,+−−are diﬀerent zero. For these amplitudes we found in the leading approximation with respect to η: M1,++−= ime3χ3 √ 2π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0)n −i(z1 + z3) S2χ2 +S  z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1 + ν2z3 + ν3z2t2 3 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 3)  o + G(1) + G(−ν3) ; M1,−−−= ime3χ3 √ 2π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0)n −i(z2 + z3) S2χ2 +S  z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1t2 1 + ν2z3 + ν3z2 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 1)  o + G(−ν2) + G(−ν3) ; M3,+−−= ime3χ3 √ 2π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0) ×S  z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1t2 1 + ν2z3t2 2 + ν3z2t2 3 + 2ν2ν3t1t2t3  . (39) Here we introduced the following quantities: G(ν) = ν me3χ 2 √ 2π2 Z ∞ 0 ds Z 1 0 du [1 −2u(1 −u)]e−i(s+ψ1) ; z1 = 2x1(x2ν2 + x3ν3) + 2x2x3 −1 , z2 = 2x1(−x2ν2 + x3ν3) −2x2x3 + ν2 , z3 = 2x1(x2ν2 −x3ν3) −2x2x3 + ν3 , xj = sj S , tj = 1 −2xj , ψ0 = 4 3χ2S3[x2 2x2 3 + 2x1x2x3(ν2x2 + ν3x3) + x2 1(ν2x2 −ν3x3)2] , ψ1 = 4 3ν2χ2u2(1 −u)2s3 . (40) 15 The amplitude M1,+−+ is obtained from M1,++−by the replacement ν2 ↔ν3. Equations (39) are independent of the parameter η and are valid for arbitrary values of χ. The linear terms of the expansion of these amplitudes with respect to χ vanish. Then, the expansion starts with the terms proportional to χ3 that coincide with Eqs. (38). As it is known, both the electromagnetic invariants FµνF µν and ǫµνρσF µνF ρσ are equal to zero for a plane wave, F µν being the tensor of the electromagnetic ﬁeld. The Euler-Heisenberg Lagrangian depends on these two invariants but not on χ which in our case has the form χ = p −(eFµνkν 1)2/(2m3). Therefore only the terms (38) can be obtained by means of this eﬀective Lagrangian but not the total answer (39). Thus, the applicability of the Euler-Heisenberg Lagrangian to the calculation of the photon splitting amplitudes is restricted by the conditions η ≪1 and χ ≪1. It is worth noting that the region of applicability of the small-χ asymptotics is very narrow. For instance, at χ = 0.1 the diﬀerence between the leading terms (38) and the exact result (39) is already around 20%. We illustrate the importance of high-order terms in χ in Fig. 2 where we show the total photon splitting rate ˙W at intermediate values of the parameter χ. The solid line is obtained by using the amplitudes (39), the dashed line by employing Eq. (38). At ω1 ≫m it is very diﬃcult to measure the energy of the ﬁnal photons with the accuracy necessary to distinguish between the process of photon splitting with one-photon absorption and three-photon absorption. However, there is another possibility to distinguish these two processes which is based on the use of the angular distribution of the ﬁnal photons. At ω1 ≫ω0, the ﬁnal photons are almost parallel to the initial one so that ϑ2,3 ≪1 where ϑ2 = ∠(k2, k1) and ϑ3 = ∠(k3, k1). It follows from Eq. (31) that d ˙Wn, σ1σ2σ3 = π nω0 |Mn, σ1σ2σ3|2 ϑ2 dϑ2 dφq  1 + ω1 4nω0 ϑ2 2 2 . (41) Using the expressions (39), we have tabulated the ratio R = P σj d ˙W1, σ1σ2σ3 P σj(d ˙W1, σ1σ2σ3 + d ˙W3, σ1σ2σ3) . (42) The result of this tabulation at χ = 1 is shown in Fig. 3. It can be seen that R noticeably diﬀers from unity at angles ϑ2 such that ϑ2 ≫ p ω0/ω1. It is interesting to compare the amplitudes of photon splitting in a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld with those in a linearly polarized ﬁeld. In the latter case for η ≪1 and ﬁxed χ, 16 the leading terms of the amplitudes are not zero for all odd n. This is due to the fact that a linearly polarized laser beam contains photons with both Jz = +1 and Jz = −1. In this sense the situation reminds us of the properties of the polarization operator in the laser ﬁeld [30]. In the case of a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld it is convenient to perform the calculations of the amplitudes in terms of the linear polarization of photons. Since the vectors k2 and k3 are almost parallel to k1, the azimuth asymmetry disappears. Therefore, we can choose A(φ) = a1A1 cos(ω0φ) and we have χ = ηξ1/ √ 2 with ξ1 = eA1/m. The polarization of each photon is parallel either to a1 or to a2 so that our amplitudes coincide with the coeﬃcients Rλ1λ2λ3 in Eq. (9). Note that a2 = κ × a1 and thus a2 is a pseudo-vector. Therefore, the nonzero amplitudes in the collinear approximation are those which either do not contain or contain twice the indices λ = 2, namely, Mn,111, Mn,122, Mn,212, and Mn,221. Putting ρn ∝√η equal to zero in Eqs. (20) and (25) and performing the integral over φ, we obtain the leading contributions of the amplitudes at η ≪1 for a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld Mn,111 = i n+3 2 √ 2me3χ3 π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0)  −i(z1 + z2 + z3)D(1) n (ψ0) 2S2χ2 +SD(2) n (ψ0)  2z1z2z3 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 1)z1 + ν2(1 + t2 2)z3 + ν3(1 + t2 3)z2 −4ν2ν3 + ν2ν3(1 + t1)(1 + t2)(1 + t3)  + eGn(1) + eGn(−ν2) + eGn(−ν3) ; Mn,122 = i n+3 2 √ 2me3χ3 π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0)  −iz1D(1) n (ψ0) 2S2χ2 −SD(2) n (ψ0)ν2ν3(1 −t1)  (1 + t1)z1 + 1 −t2t3  + eGn(1) ; Mn,212 = i n+3 2 √ 2me3χ3 π2 Z ∞ Z 0 Z ds1 ds2 ds3 e−i(S+ψ0)  −iz2D(1) n (ψ0) 2S2χ2 +SD(2) n (ψ0)ν3(1 −t3)  (1 + t3)z2 −ν2(1 −t1t2)  + eGn(−ν2) , (43) 17 where D(1) n (y) = J n−1 2 (y) −iJ n+1 2 (y) , D(2) n (y) =  1 + in −1 4y  J n−1 2 (y) −i  1 −in + 1 4y  J n+1 2 (y) , eGn(ν) = ν i n+1 2 me3χ 2 √ 2π2 Z ∞ 0 ds Z 1 0 du [1 −2u(1 −u)]e−i(s+ψ1)D(1) n (ψ1) , (44) and the other notations are presented in Eq. (40). The amplitudes Mn,221 are obtained from Mn,212 by the replacement ν2 ↔ν3. In Fig. 4 we show the photon splitting rate ˙W in a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld as a function of the number n of laser photons absorbed. The rate has been calculated from the above amplitudes (43) with χ = 1. The ﬁgure shows that, as the number n increases, the photon splitting rate decreases quite rapidly and in a non-perturbative way. The leading contributions to the amplitudes at χ ≪1 have the form [see also Eq. (38)] M1,111 = 24 5 e N , M3,111 = −8 5 e N , M1,122 = M1,212 = 13 5 e N M3,122 = M3,212 = −13 15 e N . (45) Note that at η ≪1 there is a simple relation between the amplitudes of the photon splitting in a crossed ﬁeld and those calculated in a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld [11]. The amplitudes in a crossed ﬁeld depend only on the parameter χ while those in a laser ﬁeld depend only on χ in the leading approximation for η ≪1. In this case, it can easily be shown that M(lin) n,λ1λ2λ3(χ) = Z 2π 0 dφ 2πeinφM(cross) λ1λ2λ3(χ sin φ) . (46) Therefore, due to Parseval’s identity, we have X n |M(lin) n,λ1λ2λ3(χ)|2 = Z 2π 0 dφ 2π|M(cross) λ1λ2λ3(χ sin φ)|2 . (47) For the same reason, in the case of a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld the following relation holds X n, σi |M(circ) n,σ1σ2σ3(χ)|2 = X σi |M(cross) σ1σ2σ3(χ)|2 , (48) where the indices σi denote the helicities of the external photons. We emphasize that Eq. (48) is not valid without the summation over σi. This is because the rotational symmetry 18 around the vector κ is absent in a crossed ﬁeld (see discussion below Eq. (27)), as is the conservation of the projection of the total angular momentum Jz. Therefore, it is impossible to obtain the amplitudes M(circ) n,σ1σ2σ3(χ) in a circularly polarized ﬁeld from those in a crossed ﬁeld by using any Fourier transformation as it was done in Eq. (46). Now, at χ ≫1 the asymptotics (39) obtained for the circularly polarized ﬁeld read M1,++−= Nc ( Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3  4(z1 + z3) ˜ψ + z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1 +ν2z3 + ν3z2t2 3 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 3)  + 8Γ2(1/3) 5Γ(2/3) (1 −ν1/3 3 ) ) ; M1,−−−= Nc ( Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3  4(z2 + z3) ˜ψ + z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1t2 1 +ν2z3 + ν3z2 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 1)  −8Γ2(1/3) 5Γ(2/3) (ν1/3 2 + ν1/3 3 ) ) ; M3,+−−= Nc Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3 ␀z1z2z3 −ν2ν3z1t2 1 + ν2z3t2 2 + ν3z2t2 3 + 2ν2ν3t1t2t3  ,(49) where Nc = me3(6χ)1/3e−iπ/6Γ(1/3) 24 √ 2π2 ; ˜ψ = x2 2x2 3 + 2x1x2x3(ν2x2 + ν3x3) + x2 1(ν2x2 −ν3x3)2 , x1 = 1 −u , x2 = uw , x3 = u(1 −w) , (50) and zj and tj are expressed via xj as in Eq. (40). Analogously, at χ ≫1 we ﬁnd from Eq. 19 (43) for a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld Mn,111 = Nn * Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3  4(z1 + z2 + z3) ˜ψ + 2z1z2z3 −ν2ν3(1 + t2 1)z1 + ν2(1 + t2 2)z3 +ν3(1 + t2 3)z2 −4ν2ν3 + ν2ν3(1 + t1)(1 + t2)(1 + t3)  + 8Γ2(1/3) 5Γ(2/3) (1 −ν1/3 2 −ν1/3 3 ) + ; Mn,122 = Nn * Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3  4z1 ˜ψ −ν2ν3(1 −t1)  (1 + t1)z1 + 1 −t2t3  + 8Γ2(1/3) 5Γ(2/3) + ; Mn,212 = Nn * Z 1 0 du u Z 1 0 dw ˜ψ4/3  4z2 ˜ψ + ν3(1 −t3)  (1 + t3)z2 −ν2(1 −t1t2)  −8Γ2(1/3) 5Γ(2/3) ν1/3 2 + , Nn = me3(6χ)1/3eiπ/3Γ(1/6)Γ(n/2 −1/6) 25/672π5/2Γ(n/2 + 7/6) . (51) The dependence on n in these expressions is contained in the coeﬃcients Nn which at large n rapidly decrease as n−4/3. Instead, the dependence of the amplitudes on the parameter χ, both for a linearly and a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld, is weak and proportional to χ1/3. C. Amplitudes for large η and ﬁxed ξ = χ/η The structure of the photon splitting amplitudes at large η and ﬁxed ξ = χ/η is rather interesting because the function G(ν) introduced in Eq. (34) and which enters the contri- butions M(2) n,σ1σ2σ3 has the form G(ν) = δn1 imξe3 8 √ 2π2  log2 4η(−ν −i0) 1 + ξ2  −4 −4iλ Z ∞ 0 dϑsin ϑ eiλϑ ϑ log  1 + ξ2 1 + ξ2(1 −sin2 ϑ/ϑ2)  , (52) with λ = sign(ν), and therefore contains diﬀerent powers of log η as well as some functions of ξ2. However, when we add the corresponding asymptotics of M(3) 1,σ1σ2σ3 which is the leading term in the limiting case under discussion, any dependence on η and ξ is cancelled out and we obtain the high-energy asymptotics of photon-photon scattering in vacuum [34]. Therefore, analogously to the case of photon-photon scattering in vacuum, the high-energy asymptotics of the amplitudes of photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld is independent of the energy of the initial photon as well as of the parameter ξ. More precisely, these statements are valid for any value of ξ which satisﬁes the inequality ξ2 ≪η. 20 VII. POSSIBILITY OF EXPERIMENTAL OBSERVATION OF PHOTON SPLIT- TING IN A LASER FIELD We now discuss the possibility of experimental observation of photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld. It is natural to consider the principal scheme of the experiment which was used in the ﬁrst successful observation of photon splitting in a Coulomb ﬁeld [7]. In this experiment a tagged photon beam was used and the energy of each ﬁnal photon was measured by a calorimeter. It was necessary to conﬁrm two-photon events, and the distance d between photons in the calorimeter was about one centimeter. Assuming a distance l ≈25 m between the calorimeter and the interaction region between tagged photons and laser ﬁeld, then the angles ϑ2 = ∠(k2, k1) and ϑ3 = ∠(k3, k1) have to be larger than ϑ0 = 4 × 10−4 rad. At ω1 ≫ω0 the ﬁnal photons are almost parallel to the initial one so that ϑ2,3 ≪1. It follows from Eq. (30) that in this limit ϑ2 = 2 r Ωnν3 ω1ν2 , ϑ3 = 2 r Ωnν2 ω1ν3 . (53) If, for instance, ν2 > ν3, then for n = 1 ω1 < 4ν3ω0 ν2ϑ2 0 = 25 × 106 ν3ω0 ν2 . (54) For the planned photon energy of the X-FEL at DESY [26], with ω0 = 1 keV, the latter condition becomes ω1 < 25ν3/ν2 GeV and is not restrictive. Then, we can essentially reduce l or ω1, or increase ϑ0. The planned intensity of the X-FEL is about 2 × 1015 W/cm2 corresponding to E/Ec ≈10−7 and ξ ≈5 × 10−5. In this case, the amplitudes of photon splitting coincide with those of photon-photon scattering. Therefore, it is convenient to choose ω1 = m2/ω0 = 250 MeV for which η = 1. This value of η corresponds to the threshold of electron-positron pair production and therefore the background initiated by this process will be strongly suppressed. Besides, at η = 1 the cross section of photon- photon scattering is almost maximal and is given by σ ∼10−30 cm2 (see e. g. [16] on pg. 572). Now, from Eq. (33) we ﬁnd ˙W ≈60 sec−1. The X-FEL pulses will have a duration of about 100 fs and the interval between pulses will be about 93 ns. If we take, for example, a ﬂux of 108 tagged photons per second [35], we obtain about two photon splitting events per hour. However, the very small laser beam-size makes this estimation essentially smaller. In consequence, the possibility of experimental observation of photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld becomes problematic when using the X-FEL and the scheme of Ref. [7]. 21 We discuss now the possibility of using a very strong optical laser as that described in [25]. In that case ω0 = 1 eV, and the laser intensity will be of order of 1025 W/cm2 corresponding to E/Ec ≈4.6 × 10−3. For a value of the angle ϑ0 = 10−4 we obtain, e. g. for ν3/ν2 = 1/4, approximately 100 MeV as the upper limit for ω1. For these parameters we ﬁnd η = 4 × 10−4 and χ = 0.9. Therefore, we have to use Eq. (39) but not the asymptotics (38) because high-order corrections in χ are important. Using the numerical results shown in Fig. 2 we obtain ˙W = 4 × 108 sec−1 at χ = 0.9. Though the value of ˙W is very large, the duration of each pulse is 10 fs and the laser repetition rate is only 1 Hz [25]. For a total ﬂux of 108 photons per second [35] and for an electron bunch revolution frequency equal to 1 MHz in the accelerator, we obtain an average number of tagged photons per pulse equal to 100. If all the photons pass through the laser pulse, we obtain again approximately two events per hour. However, the transverse beam size of a focused optical laser is even smaller than that of an X-FEL and the reduction due to the tagged and laser beams overlapping is larger. Our negative conclusion about the possibility of observing photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld when using the scheme discussed is mainly due to the short duration and small size of the laser beam as well as to the small ﬂux of tagged photons. On the one hand, short and focused laser beams are closely coupled with large intensity of the laser pulses. On the other hand, the use of tagged photons, which essentially restricts the statistics, is needed to suppress the large background due to many other processes (double Compton scattering with the experimental equipment like collimators, bremsstrahlung accompanied by e+ −e− pair production and so on). Of course, we cannot exclude the existence of other schemes of the experiment that avoid the diﬃculties pointed out in this Section. VIII. CONCLUSIONS In conclusion, we have derived compact expressions for the amplitudes of the photon splitting process in a plane wave of arbitrary form, intensity and polarization (see Eqs. (20) and (25)). In the simpler case of a monochromatic circularly polarized laser ﬁeld, the amplitudes have the form of three-fold integrals (see Eqs. (34) and (36)). Moreover, we analyzed in detail various asymptotics of the amplitudes in the case of a monochromatic wave with linear or circular polarization. We have demonstrated that the angular distribution 22 of the probabilities corresponding to diﬀerent numbers of absorbed photons is noticeably diﬀerent; this permits us the distinction between these processes for the case where the laser photon energy is less than the accuracy of the ﬁnal photon energy measurement. The photon splitting amplitudes depend on the two Lorentz-invariant parameters η and χ. For arbitrary values of these parameters, the amplitudes both in the case of a circularly and a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld are not equal to zero for any (odd) number n of absorbed laser photons. This is because the angle between the two ﬁnal photons is not zero and the conservation of the projection Jz of the total angular momentum (in the case of a circularly polarized laser ﬁeld) does not imply any restriction. However, we have shown that in the limit of small η the leading contribution to the amplitudes for a circularly polarized laser comes from the terms with n = 1 and n = 3, while for a linearly polarized laser ﬁeld the leading contributions contain the terms with any odd n. Finally, we have demonstrated that the scheme previously employed in the experiment of photon splitting in an atomic ﬁeld together with new generation X-FEL sources and strong optical lasers is not suitable for the corresponding experiment in a laser ﬁeld. Therefore, it is necessary to ﬁnd another more appropriate experimental conﬁguration to observe photon splitting in a laser ﬁeld. Acknowledgments We would like to thank K. Z. Hatsagortsyan for valuable discussions. A. I. M. gratefully acknowledges the great hospitality and the ﬁnancial support he has received during his visit at Max-Planck-Institute for Nuclear Physics. The work was supported in part by the RFBR under grants 0502-16079 and 06-02-04018. APPENDIX: COEFFICIENTS FOR THE HELICITY AMPLITUDES Here we present the coeﬃcients Bj,σ1σ2σ3 used in Eq. (36). We introduce the abbreviations xj = sj S , d1 = (τ1 −