l Poincar´e sphere (parameterised by the spherical angles θ and ϕ) and ˆL is an angular momentum operator, form Schmidt bases for (A7). The components of the angular momentum op- erator: ˆLx, ˆLy, and ˆLz are the three SU(2) generators. Of these, only ˆLz represents real spatial rotations along the propagation of light, and it is thus the only compo- nent associated with the orbital angular momentum of light that can be measured in experiments [20]. APPENDIX B In this second Appendix we explicitly show how to cal- culate the radial functions Rℓ(s) given in Eq. (5). For the chosen two-photon amplitude (4) and the measure- ment scenario, these radial functions depend on the spa- tial overlap of the Schmidt and the ﬁber (fundamental Gaussian) modes at the planes where the azimuthal phase plates are located. We need to evaluate the integrals Rℓ= P n √λℓn R rirsdridrsv(wG) ℓA=0,nA=0(ri)v(wG) ℓB=0,nB=0(rs) ×v(wS) ℓ,n (ri)v(wS) −ℓ,n(rs). We use the Schmidt eigenvalues λℓn = (1 −ξ2)2ξ2|ℓ|+4n found in Appendix A. The idler and signal Schmidt modes v(wS) ℓ,n (ri) and v(wS) −ℓ,n(rs) be- long to the Laguerre-Gaussian mode basis. We stress once again the fact that the corresponding ﬁber wG and Schmidt wS widths are not necessarily equal. This im- plies that the orthogonality relation for these modes, R rdr v(wG) ℓG,nG(r)v(wS) ℓS,nS(r) = δℓGℓSδnGnS, does not hold in general when wS ̸= wG. We start by recalling a remarkable identity between Laguerre polynomials L|ℓ| n and the modiﬁed Bessel func- tions Iℓ[38] ∞ X n=0 n!L|ℓ| n (x)L|ℓ| n (y) Γ(|ℓ| + n + 1) zn = (xyz)−|ℓ|/2 1 −z exp  −(x + y)z 1 −z  × Iℓ 2√xyz 1 −z  , (A8) valid for all real x, y, integer ℓand complex z (|z| < 1). In detail, the integrals to calculate read Rℓ= (1 −ξ2)ξ|ℓ| ∞ X n=0 ξ2n Z ∞ 0 ridri 2 e − r2 i w2 G wG  n! (|ℓ| + n)!  1 2 × ""√ 2ri wS #|ℓ| L|ℓ| n 2r2 i w2 S  2e − r2 i w2 S wS Z ∞ 0 rsdrs 2e − r2 s w2 G wG ×  n! (|ℓ| + n)!  1 2 ""√ 2rs wS #|ℓ| L|ℓ| n 2r2 s w2 S  2e − r2 s w2 S wS . (A9) Inserti