 · ⃗βu) −⃗βu] r′ (A.14) 31 where (5.13) has been used. (A.12)-(A.14) together with (5.15) then give −Q⃗βu cr′ ∂ ∂t  1 K  = −Q⃗βu K3    (ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u) (r′)2 + ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu cr′   . (A.15) Collecting together (A.7), (A.10), (A.11) and (A.15) gives, for the electric ﬁeld derived from the LW potentials: ⃗E = Q K2(r′)2   ˆr′ −⃗βu[1 + (ˆr′ · ⃗βu)] − ⃗βu[(ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u] + ˆr′[ˆr′ · ⃗βu −β2 u] K    − Q K2cr′  ˙⃗βu + (⃗βu −ˆr′)ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu K   = Q K3  ˆr′ −⃗βu γ2u(r′)2 + ˆr′ × [(ˆr′ −βu) × ˙⃗βu] cr′  . (A.16) The retarded LW magnetic ﬁeld given by (A.6) is now calculated. Since ⃗u is assumed to be parallel to the x-axis, it follows that −Q K ⃗βu × ⃗∇  1 r′  = −Qˆkβu K ∂ ∂yq  1 r′  = Qˆkβu K(r′)2 ∂r′ ∂yq = Qˆkβuyq K2(r′)3 = Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) K2(r′)2 . (A.17) Similarly −Q K ⃗βu × ⃗∇  1 K  = −Qˆkβu K ∂ ∂yq  1 K  = −Qˆkβu K2r′ ∂(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) ∂yq = −Qˆkβu K2r′  ˆr′ · ∂⃗βu ∂yq + ⃗βu · ∂ˆr′ ∂yq  . (A.18) Evaluating the ﬁrst term in brackets on the right side of Eq.(A.18), ˆr′ · ∂⃗βu ∂yq = ∂t′ ∂yq (ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) = −yq(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) cKr′ (A.19) where the relation ∂t′ ∂yq = −1 c ∂r′ ∂yq (A.20) 32 given by diﬀerentiating the retardation condition t′ = t −r′/c as well as Eq.(5.18) have been used. The second tern in brackets on the right side of(A.18) is ⃗βu · ∂ˆr′ ∂yq = ⃗βu · ∂ ∂yq ⃗r′ r′ ! = ⃗βu · 1 r′ ∂⃗r′ ∂yq − ⃗r′ (r′)2 ∂r′ ∂yq ! . (A.21) Assuming, without loss of generality, that the vector ⃗r′ is conﬁned to the x −y plane, ∂⃗r′ ∂yq = −ˆıdxQ dt′ ∂t′ ∂yq + ˆ. (A.22) Combining (A.21) and (A.22), again using (A.20) and (5.18), gives ⃗βu · ∂ˆr′ ∂yq = [β2 u −ˆr′ · ⃗βu]yq K(r′)2 . (A.23) Combining A(18), (A.19) and (A.23), −Q K ⃗βu × ⃗∇  1 K  = Q⃗βu × ˆr′ K3  [ˆr′ · ⃗βu −β2 u] (r′)2 + ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu cr′   (A.24) The third term on the right side of (A.6) is Q Kr′(⃗∇× ⃗βu) = −Qˆk Kr′ ∂βu ∂yq = −Qˆk Kr′ ∂t′ ∂yq ˙βu = Qˆk cKr′ ∂r′ ∂yq ˙βu = Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) cK2r′βu ˙βu (A.25) where (A.20) and (5.18) have been used. Collecting together (A.17), (A.24) and (A.25), the magnetic ﬁeld generated by the LW potentials is: ⃗B =    Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) K3  K + ˆr′ · ⃗βu −β2 u (r′)2 + K ˙βu + βu(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) cr′βu      t′=t′ Q , =    Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) K3   1 γ2u(r′)2 + ( ˙βu(1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu) + βu(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) cr′βu      t′=t′ Q . (A.26) (A.16) and (A.26) are the formulae (6.6) and (6.7) of the text. The consistency of the ﬁelds of Eqs.(6.44) and (6.45) with the LW ﬁelds of (6.6) and (6.7) claimed in Ref. [37] is now investigated. The equations analogous to (A.5) and (A.6) given by using the chain rule to expand the derivatives in (6.44) and (6.45) are: ⃗E = Qˆr′ K(r′)2 + Qˆr′ cr′ ∂ ∂t  1 K  + Qˆr′ cK ∂ ∂t  1 r′  + Q cKr′ ∂ˆr′ ∂t −Q⃗βu cK ∂ ∂t  1 r′  −Q⃗βu cr′ ∂ ∂t  1 K  − Q cKr′ ∂⃗βu ∂t , (A.27) ⃗B = Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) K(r′)2 + Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) cr′ ∂ ∂t  1 K  + Q(⃗βu × ˆr′) cK ∂ ∂t  1 r′  + Q cKr′ ⃗βu × ∂ˆr′ ∂t ! − Q cKr′  ˆr′ × ∂⃗βu ∂t  . (A.28) 33 . Comparison with (A.5) and (A.6) shows that the last three terms in (A.5) and (A.27) (originating from the time derivative in (A.3)) are the same but all other terms in (A.27) and (A.28) diﬀer from those in (A.5) and (A.6). Thus to compare (A.5) and (A.27) the derivatives in the second. third and fourth terms on the right of (A.27) must be evaluated, while to compare (A.6) and (A.28) all derivatives on the right side of (A.28) must be evaluated. This is readily done using, mutatis mutandis, the formulae obtained above. The second term in (A.27) is Qˆr′ cr′ ∂ ∂t  1 K  = Qˆr′ cKr′ ∂ ∂t′  1 K  = Qˆr′ K3    (ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u) (r′)2 + ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu cr′    (A.29) by analogy with Eq.(A.15). The third term is Qˆr′ cK ∂ ∂t  1 r′  = Qˆr′ cK2 ∂ ∂t′  1 r′  = − Qˆr′ cK2(r′)2 ∂r′ ∂t′ = Qˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) K2(r′)2 (A.30) where (5.15) and (5.13) have been used The fourth term is Q cKr′ ∂ˆr′ ∂t = Q cK2r′ ∂ˆr′ ∂t′ = Q[ˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) −⃗βu] K2(r′)2 . (A.31) Substituting (A.29)-(A.31) into (A.22) as well as the previously obtained terms, and performing some algebraic simpliﬁcation, gives ⃗E = Q K3  ˆr′ −⃗βu γ2 u(r′)2 + ˆr′ × [(ˆr′ −βu) × ˙⃗βu] cr′  . (A.32) Which is the LW ﬁeld of Eq.(A.16) Noting that the ﬁrst three terms of (A.28) diﬀer from those of (A.27) by the replace- ment ˆr′ →⃗βu × ˆr′ and using the above results for the latter terms, gives, after algebraic simpliﬁcation, the LW magnetic ﬁeld of Eq. (6.7). 34 Appendix B For clarity the total time derivatives in (6.6) are replaced the corresponding partial derivatives with respect to the present time, t, for a ﬁxed value of the ﬁeld point position ⃗xq. The second term on the right side of (6.10) contains the derivative: ∂ ∂t ˆr′ (r′)2 ! = ∂t′ ∂t ∂ ∂t′ ⃗r′ (r′)3 ! = ∂t′ ∂t "" 1 (r′)3 ∂⃗r′ ∂t′ −3⃗r′ (r′)4 ∂r′ ∂t′ # . (B.1) Since the vector ⃗r′ is conﬁned to the x-y plane, ∂⃗r′ ∂t′ = ˆı∂(xq −xQ) ∂t′ + ˆ∂yq ∂t′ = −c⃗βu (B.2) since ∂yq/∂t′ = 0 and cβu = dxQ/dt. (5.13), (5.15), (B.1) and (B.2) give: r′ c ∂ ∂t ˆr′ (r′)2 ! = 1 1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu  3ˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) −⃗βu (r′)2  . (B.3) Considering now the last term on the right side of (6.10): ∂2ˆr′ ∂t2 = ∂t′ ∂t ∂ ∂t′ ""∂t′ ∂t ∂ˆr′ ∂t′ # = ∂t′ ∂t ""∂ˆr′ ∂t′ ∂ ∂t′ ∂t′ ∂t ! + ∂t′ ∂t ∂2ˆr′ ∂t′2 # (B.4) where ∂ ∂t′ ∂t′ ∂t ! = ∂ ∂t′ 1 1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu ! = 1 (1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 ∂(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) ∂t′ = 1 (1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2  c[(ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u] r′ + (ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu)   (B.5) where (A.14) has been used. It also follows from (A.14) that ∂2ˆr′ ∂t′2 = −c[ˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) −⃗βu] (r′)2 ∂r′ ∂t′ + c r′ "" ∂ˆr′ ∂t′ ! (ˆr′ · ⃗βu) + ˆr′ ∂ˆr′ ∂t′ ! · ⃗βu + ˆr′(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) −˙⃗βu # = c2  ˆr′[3(ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u] −2⃗βu(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) (r′)2  + c[ˆr′(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) −˙⃗βu] r′ . (B.6) Combining (5.15) and (A.14),(B.5) and (B.6) then gives 1 c2 ∂2ˆr′ ∂t2 = 1 (1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu)    [ˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) −⃗βu] (1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2  ˆr′(ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u (r′)2 + ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu cr′   + 1 (1 −ˆr′ · ⃗βu)  ˆr′[3(ˆr′ · ⃗βu)2 −β2 u] −2⃗βu(ˆr′ · ⃗βu) (r′)2 + ˆr′(ˆr′ · ˙⃗βu) −˙⃗βu cr′     .(B.7) Inserting (B.3) and (B.7) into (6.10) yields Eq.(6.12) of the text. 35 References [1] J.H.Field, Phys. Scr. 74 702 (2006), http://